<title>RUSSIA: Chinese grain imports to stay modest - Goldthwait.</title>
<headline>Chinese grain imports to stay modest - Goldthwait.</headline>
<dateline>MOSCOW 1997-04-04</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.S. Agriculture Department (USDA) General Sales Manager Chris Goldthwait, echoing comments made a day earlier by the USDA Secretary, said on Friday that Chinese grain imports would stay modest this year.</p>
<p>&quot;I think you would probably anticipate imports of at least a little higher level in the coming year, because of increasing demand year-by-year,&quot; Goldthwait said in a brief telephone interview in Moscow.</p>
<p>&quot;China is coming off a very good grain crop this past season -- they had excellent production of both wheat and feed grains, which has enabled them to import what is for China a relatively modest level of grain this season,&quot; he said.</p>
<p>USDA Secretary Dan Glickman said on Thursday that he did not expect China to become a major grain importer any time soon.</p>
<p>--Moscow Newsroom, +7095 941 8520</p>
